## Exports (goods/services; note ↑/↓ if stated)
- U.S. services surplus strengthened in February 2015, despite overall trade deficit [Source: obamawhitehouse.archives.gov];  
- U.S. merchandise trade surplus with the rest of the world increased from $1.3 billion in 1953 to $2.7 billion in 1955 [Source: www.imf.org];  
- U.S. trade deficit in 2014 increased, raising concerns about future trade agreements [Source: www.epi.org];  
- U.S. maintains strong and growing surplus in services [Source: obamawhitehouse.archives.gov];  
- U.S. has 17 free trade agreements (FTAs) with other countries, and trade balance for goods improved after implementation [Source: www.thirdway.org];  
- International visitors to the U.S. totaled nearly $3.3 billion in February 2015, contributing to trade balance [Source: 2010-2014.commerce.gov];  
- The International Trade Commission (ITC) estimated that tariff reductions in the Trade Promotion Agreement would expand exports of U.S. goods [Source: ustr.gov];  

## Imports
- U.S. global trade deficit is bloated, with Japan responsible for a substantial share [Source: www.epi.org];  
- U.S. trade balance deteriorated, ceding global leadership in advanced industries [Source: www.brookings.edu];  

## Bilateral / region
- China's merchandise surplus is largely with the U.S., and its services deficit is expected to continue growing [Source: www.piie.com];  
- Germany had a total export of 1,635,599,573.79 in thousands of US dollars, with a trade growth of 9.15% compared to the previous year [Source: wits.worldbank.org];  
- Australia had a total export of 342,036,103.27 in thousands of US dollars, with a trade growth of 18.90% compared to the previous year [Source: wits.worldbank.org];  

## Policy / tariff / sanctions
- The Office of the United States Trade Representative publishes a yearly National Trade Estimate Report on Foreign Trade Barriers [Source: www.europarl.europa.eu];  
- The World Trade Organization (WTO) governs global trade rules, and the U.S. is a member [Source: www.wto.org];  

## Official prints (Census/BEA)
- U.S. merchandise trade surplus with the rest of the world increased from $1.3 billion in 1953 to $2.7 billion in 1955 [Source: www.imf.org];  

## Noise
- U.S. trade deficit in 2014 increased, warning against signing trade agreements that could worsen the trade balance [Source: www.epi.org];
